# GAME OVER - played once, slowly, and it ends.
# The format is in Demo1/CLAUDE.md, under "Song files". Instruments are tables with
# one entry per frame; each channel holds named tracks of sixteen rows; the lines at the
# end are the order list. In a track '.' holds and '-' releases. '#' starts a comment.
#
# A lead slipping down by half-steps - A, G#, G, F, E - onto D, over D minor, B-flat,
# A major and D minor again: i, VI, V, i, the saddest short cadence there is. A low
# thud lands on the final chord, and 'end' lets it all fade.
#
# A release can be no slower than one volume step a frame, which is a quarter of a second
# from full. A sad ending wants longer than that, so the long fade is in the volume
# tables themselves: every instrument here decays for as long as its note is held, and
# the release only finishes what is already quiet.

song   GAME OVER
key    D MINOR

# A sigh: the volume creeps in over five frames, then falls away for as long as the note
# is held. Vibrato arrives late, on the notes long enough to carry it.
instrument SIGH
  table      tone
  volume     4 7 9 11 12 12 12 12 11 11 11 11 11 10 10 10 10 10 9 9 9 9 9 8 8 8 8 8 7 7 7 7 7 7 6 6 6 6 6 6 5 5 5 5 5
  distortion tone
  vibrato    0 1 1 0 -1 -1
  delay      24
  release    1

# The chords, arpeggiated and fading from the moment they are struck.
instrument DIRGE
  table      tone
  volume     9 9 8 8 8 7 7 7 7 6 6 6 6 6 5 5 5 5 4 4 4
  distortion tone
  release    1

instrument LOW
  table      buzz
  volume     7 9 10 10 10 9 9 9 8 8 8 8 7 7 7 7 7 6 6 6 6 6 5
  distortion buzz
  release    1

# The last thing heard: distortion C falling from 69 Hz to 34 as it dies.
instrument DOOM
  table      none
  volume     13 12 10 8 6 4 2 1 0
  distortion buzz
  audf       60 70 80 90 100 110 120 130
  release    2

channel 1 drums DOOM
  note  One sound, once: a low thud on the final chord. A game over is quiet until it is
  note  final.
  kit   D=DOOM
  track LAST  . . . .  . . . .  D . . .  . . . .

channel 2 notes BASS
  note  D, B-flat, A, D on the buzz table - every one within 11 cents - swelling in and
  note  fading like everything else here.
  voice LOW
  track I-VI  D2 . . .  . . . .  A#1 . . .  . . . .
  track V-I   A1 . . .  . . . .  D2 . . .  . . . .

channel 3 notes LEAD
  note  Half-steps down: A, G#, G, F, E, D. A chromatic fall is the oldest way music has of
  note  saying something has run out. No vibrato until a note has lasted, so the short
  note  ones stay plain.
  voice SIGH
  track FALL  A4 . . .  . . . .  G#4 . . .  . . . .
  track REST  G4 . F4 .  E4 . . .  D4 . . .  . . . .

channel 4 notes CHORDS
  note  D minor, B-flat, A major and D minor - the V is major, so its C# pulls hard toward
  note  the last chord before it arrives.
  voice DIRGE
  track I-VI  Dm . . .  . . . .  Bb . . .  . . . .
  track V-I   A . . .  . . . .  Dm . . .  . . . .

# The order list: drums, bass, lead, chords. It ends rather than loops.
speed  10
audctl $00
line   .      I-VI   FALL   I-VI
line   LAST   V-I    REST   V-I
end
